{
  "case_id": "2012-12-307",
  "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court Clerk",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:18 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0000-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We will hear argument this morning in Case 12-307, United States v. Windsor, and we will begin with the jurisdictional discussion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Vicki C. Jackson",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is no justiciable case before this Court. Petitioner, the United States, does not ask this Court to redress the injuries it asserts. The House of Representatives' Bipartisan Legal Advisory Group, the BLAG, which does seek redress in the form of reversal, asserts no judicially cognizable injury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Who else is going to be aggrieved if she is not? Meaning another person who is -- whose benefits are withheld, tax refund is withheld, is going to be in an identical situation to her?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "$300,000 that's going to come out of the government's treasury if this decision is upheld, and it won't if it isn't. Now, the government is willing to pay that $300,000, would be happy to pay that $300,000. But whether the government is happy or sad to pay that $300,000, the government is still paying the $300,000, which in the usual set of circumstances is the classic Article III injury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: But let me ask you, suppose that constitutional scholars have grave doubts about the practice of the President signing a bill, but saying that he thinks it's, unconstitutional -- what do you call it, signing statements or something like that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Srinivasan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SRINIVASAN: I -- in the -- in the signing statement situation, Your Honor, one example in the past is Turner Broadcasting. In Turner Broadcasting, that was a circumstance in which it was -- it was a veto, but in the course of the veto the President made the determination that a particular aspect of that statute was unconstitutional. And what happened as a result of that is that the Department of Justice didn't defend that aspect of the statute in litigation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Excuse me. It's not your view. It's the President's. It's only when the President thinks it's unconstitutional that you can decline to defend it? Or what if the Attorney General thinks it's unconstitutional?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Oh, it can be either the Attorney General or the Solicitor General?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This Court not only addressed the issue of the House's standing in Chadha, it held that the House is the proper party to defend the constitutionality of an Act of Congress when the executive agency charged with its enforcement agrees with plaintiff that the statute is unconstitutional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Can you tell me where the authorization is here? I know that there is a statute that gives the Senate specifically authorization to intervene. And there was consideration of extending that right to the House. But the appointment of BLAG is strange to me because it's not in a statute, it's in a House rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Paul D. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: Well, Justice Sotomayor, I can point you to two places. One is the House rules that are pursuant to the rulemaking authority and approved by the institution. They're approved in every Congress. Rule 2.8."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Paul D. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: We do. We do have a vote in H. Res. 5. At the beginning of this Congress in January, the House passed a resolution that passed, that authorized the BLAG to continue to represent the interests of the House in this particular litigation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0002-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Clement, the problem is that it would totally tort the States' decision that there is a marriage between two people, for the Federal government then to come in to say no joint return; no marital deduction; no Social Security benefits; your spouse is very sick, but you can't get leave; people -- if that set of attributes, one might well ask, what kind of marriage is this?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0002-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: There is a common law, Federal common law definition?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, it applies to over what, 1,100 Federal laws, I think we are saying."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the very fact that there are 1,100 provisions of Federal law that define the terms \"marriage\" and \"spouse\" goes a long way to showing that Federal law has not just stayed completely out of these issues. It's gotten involved in them in a variety of contexts where there is an independent Federal power that supported that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Suppose we look just at the estate tax provision that's at issue in this case, which provides specially favorable treatment to a married couple as opposed to any other individual or economic unit. What was the purpose of that? Was the purpose of that really to foster traditional marriage, or was Congress just looking for a convenient category to capture households that function as a unified economic unit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But that's begging the question because you are treating the married couples differently."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You would say it would be the same thing if the State passed a law -- Congress passes a law which says, well, there's some States -- they all used to require 18 as the age of consent. Now, a lot of them have gone to 17. So if you're 17 when you get married, then no tax deduction, no medical, no nothing."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0003-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 74,
        "start": 74
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, is what happened in 1996 -- and I'm going to quote from the House Report here -- is that \"Congress decided to reflect an honor of collective moral judgment and to express moral disapproval of homosexuality.\" Is that what happened in 1996?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0004-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 77,
        "start": 77
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CLEMENT: That would make no difference, Justice Alito. It does -- the hypothetical helpfully demonstrates, though, that when the Federal government is defining this term as it appears in the Federal Code, it is not regulating marriage as such."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 79,
        "start": 79
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: That means courts -- the courts, they do dictate in respect to time. They dictate in respect to age. They dictate in respect to all kinds of things. And what I'm looking for is what, in your opinion, is special about this homosexual marriage that would justify this, other than this kind of pure uniformity, if there is such a thing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 82,
        "start": 82
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: You think Congress can use its powers to supercede the traditional authority and prerogative of the States to regulate marriage in all respects? Congress could have a uniform definition of marriage that includes age, consanguinity, et cetera, et cetera?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 85,
        "start": 85
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Can I take you back to the example that you began with where a member of the military is injured. So let's say three soldiers are injured and they are all in same-sex relationships, and in each instance the other partner in this relationship wants to visit the soldier in -- in a hospital."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0004-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 86,
        "start": 86
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: But that's -- that's the same in the example that he just gave you, that discrimination would have been visited on the same group, and you say there it's okay."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0004-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 87,
        "start": 87
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: General, I have a question. You think, I think from your brief yesterday and today, that on some level sexual orientation should be looked at on an intermediate standard of scrutiny?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0005-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Could I ask you the same question I asked the Solicitor General? Do you think there would be a problem if Congress went the other way, the federalism problem? Obviously, you don't think there's an equal protection problem --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kaplan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0005-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "These couples are being treated as unmarried with respect to programs that affect family stability, such as the Family Leave Act, referred to by Justice Ginsburg."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kaplan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0005-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 95,
        "start": 95
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And my client was treated as unmarried when her spouse passed away, so that she had to pay $363,000 in estate taxes on the property that they had accumulated during their 44 years together."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Kaplan",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0005-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 105,
        "start": 105
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The only -- the only conclusion that can be drawn is what was in the House Report, which is moral disapproval of gay people, which the -- Congress thought was permissible in 1996 because it relied on the Court's Bowers decision, which this Court has said was wrong, not only at the time it was overruled in Lawrence, but"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Paul D. Clement",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fafccbfb71a365dc93b9b59cf7eeb152236f095103ed5d9315f8f3ce5cf99ec",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fafccbfb71a-0005-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 110,
        "start": 110
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF PAUL D. CLEMENT ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT BIPARTISAN LEGAL ADVISORY GROUP OF THE UNITED STATES"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Oral argument transcript",
      "case_id": "2012-12-307",
